Unit Purification: checker log
==============================

2026-11-14  Checker pass (stage G of spec 5.4), by an agent that did not draft the lessons
and saw none of the drafting reasoning. Scope: every primer quote, every consensus citation,
every Bidayat al-Mujtahid cross-location, every Quran ref, and the English of each claim,
position, card and check question read against the Arabic it rests on.

Mechanical
----------
- tools/verify-citations.mjs re-fetched all 73 recorded pages afresh: 160 of 160 quotes found
  on the page recorded for them (skeleton match, same normalisation as the build). Every
  position and consensus location in the lessons has a record; every difference has a
  Bidayat al-Mujtahid record; every Quran ref resolves.
- Quran refs read for sense: 5:6 (wudu, janabah, tayammum, "touched women"), 4:43
  (tayammum), 2:222 (menstruation), 25:48 and 8:11 (water that purifies), 74:4 (garments).

Position text that says more than its stored quote: re-located on the same page
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- water-01/c6 Hanafi: "other water that an impurity falls into is not used" and the flowing
  water sentence: found in al-Quduri (the first at the end of the page before the quote; the
  online copy has the typo "اوضوء" for "الوضوء" there). Confirmed.
- water-01/c6 Shafi'i: "about five hundred Baghdad ratls": found ("خمسمائة رطل بغدادي تقريبا").
- wudu-02/c2 Hanbali: the full sequence (intention, bismillah, hands, mouth and nose, face,
  arms with elbows, head with ears, feet with ankles): found in order in 'Umdat al-Fiqh.
- wudu-03/c4 Hanafi: order falls under "ويستحب للمتوضئ" in al-Quduri. Confirmed.
- wudu-03/c5 Shafi'i: al-muwalat is the last item of Abu Shuja's sunan list. Confirmed.
- ghusl-05/c4 Hanafi and Hanbali: stepping aside and washing the feet last: found in both.
- ghusl-05/c1: the early dissent about a woman's wet dream: Ibn Rushd names al-Nakha'i. Found.
- tayammum-06/c3 Maliki (to the elbows), Hanbali (more strikes or wiping further also
  count); tayammum-06/c4 Hanafi (Abu Yusuf: soil and sand only); tayammum-06/c6 Maliki
  (other views recorded): all found.
- khuff-07/c2 Hanbali (three days and nights for the traveller); khuff-07/c4 Hanafi (Abu
  Yusuf and Muhammad: "ثخينين لا يشفان الماء"); khuff-07/c5 Hanafi ("وينقض المسح ما ينقض
  الوضوء"): all found.
- najasa-08/c6 Hanafi ("وما ليس له عين مرئية"): found.

Positions worded as "not among the acts/causes the primer lists"
----------------------------------------------------------------
These state a fact about an enumerated list in the primer, not a ruling we infer. Each list
was read in full and the absent item searched for on the page(s):
- wudu-03/c5 Hanafi (continuity): al-Quduri's fard, sunan and mustahabb lists do not mention
  al-muwalat. Ibn Rushd, same mas'ala: "وذهب الشافعي وأبو حنيفة إلى أن الموالاة ليست من واجبات
  الوضوء". Consistent.
- wudu-04/c4 and c5 Hanafi (touching a woman, touching the private part): al-Quduri's list of
  what breaks wudu (which runs onto the next page: fainting, madness, laughing aloud in the
  prayer) has neither. Ibn Rushd on touching the penis: "ومنهم من لم ير فيه وضوءا أصلا وهو أبو
  حنيفة وأصحابه". Consistent.
- wudu-04/c6 Maliki and Shafi'i (bleeding): neither al-Risala's chapter nor Abu Shuja's six
  causes names blood from elsewhere in the body. Ibn Rushd attributes "the two passages"
  as the test to al-Shafi'i and his companions. Consistent for the Shafi'i entry; the Maliki
  entry rests on the primer's own list.

Findings fixed
--------------
1. water-01/c4: "every kind of natural water": "natural" is not in Ibn Rushd's sentence
   ("جميع أنواع المياه"). Removed from the claim and its card.
2. khuff-07/c1: Ibn Rushd says the condition is agreed "إلا خلافا شاذا". The claim now says
   that he notes one isolated dissent.
3. khuff-07/c2 Maliki: "No time limit" was our inference from "ما لم ينزعهما". Reworded to
   what the primer says, adding that it names no time limit (position text and school card).
4. wudu-04/c6 Hanafi: "spreads beyond its place" was loose for "فتجاوز إلى موضع يلحقه حكم
   التطهير". Now "reaches a part that purification applies to" (position text and card).

Findings answered, no change
----------------------------
- najasa-08/c5 Maliki: the quote is about water changed by something pure, not about vinegar
  by name. The position text says only what the primer says; the claim is carried by the
  Hanafi and Hanbali positions and Ibn Rushd's statement of the difference. Kept.
- wudu-04/c1: "wady, a thick white fluid that can follow urine" is a lexical gloss; the same
  description is in al-Risala's chapter ("ماء أبيض خاثر يخرج بإثر البول"). Kept.
- tayammum-06/c3 Shafi'i: the online copy of Matn Abi Shuja' drops "ومسح الوجه" from the four
  obligatory acts of tayammum (a copy error). Our position cites only "ومسح اليدين مع
  المرفقين", which is there. Kept.
- Claims of kind "differs" with fewer than four positions (wudu-03/c4, c5; ghusl-05/c6;
  khuff-07/c3, c4; najasa-08/c6): the missing primers either do not address the point in a
  quotable phrase or address it only by implication. Left out rather than inferred.

Primer citations that could not be confirmed
--------------------------------------------
None. Every shipped primer quote was located. Limits of this check: one online copy per
work (shamela.ws) was read, not a second independent print; and locating a phrase is not a
scholar's reading of it. The lessons carry "Source-checked. Not yet reviewed by a scholar."

Result: 9 of 9 lessons source-checked; 0 left as draft.

2026-11-14  Fresh review of the package (a reviewer that did not build it)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
- tools/verify-citations.mjs re-run: 160 of 160 quotes found on their recorded pages.
- Every consensus and cross-location quote was read against the claim it supports.
Findings fixed (all nine lessons raised to rev 2):
1. ghusl-05/c1: Ibn Rushd's agreed cause is "خروج المني على وجه الصحة". The claim dropped
   "على وجه الصحة" and so reported agreement on more than he does (emission through
   illness is differed on). Claim and card now say "in the ordinary, healthy way".
2. ghusl-05/c5 Maliki: "as the water is poured" was loose for "بإثر صب الماء". Now
   "straight after pouring the water" (position text and school card).
3. Check questions: the right option was the first one in all 28 questions. The options
   are reordered so the answer falls evenly across positions; no wording changed.
Noted, no change:
- The brief lists rubbing among the differences on the obligatory acts of wudu. Ibn Rushd
  treats rubbing as a difference under ghusl, not as a mas'ala of wudu, so it is carried by
  ghusl-05/c5 and wudu-03 has no rubbing claim. Left out rather than cross-located loosely.
- No hadith is cited as evidence; every claim rests on a Quran ref or a consensus citation.

2026-11-14  Independent content verification (a verifier that wrote none of the package)
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Method: all 73 recorded pages fetched afresh and read, not only searched. The four primers'
chapters (al-Quduri, al-Risala, Abu Shuja', 'Umdat al-Fiqh) and the cited entries of
Ibn al-Mundhir and Ibn Hazm were read in full; every Bidayat al-Mujtahid quote was read
with the sentences around it, looking for qualifiers the English had dropped. Each claim,
position, card and check question was then read against that Arabic.
- tools/verify-citations.mjs: all quotes found on their recorded pages, before and after
  the changes below. build-deen.mjs --check: 9 of 9 shipped.
- The unit ships no hadith item, dhikr or grading. The one hadith wording in it is the
  Maymunah narration inside the Hanbali primer's own text (ghusl-05/c4), given as the
  primer gives it.
Findings fixed (wudu-04, ghusl-05, khuff-07, najasa-08, recap-09 raised to rev 3):
1. wudu-04/c1: Ibn Rushd's agreement on urine, stool, wind, madhy and wady is "إذا كان
   خروجها على وجه الصحة". The claim dropped the condition and so reported agreement on more
   than he does (he goes on to record the difference about what leaves through illness).
   Claim and card now say "in the ordinary, healthy way". Same slip as ghusl-05/c1 earlier.
2. recap-09/c4: "What comes out of the two passages" was wider than the agreed list (Ibn
   Rushd records a difference about other things that come out, and about illness). Now
   names urine, stool and wind in the ordinary, healthy way.
3. najasa-08/c3: "the dead body of a land animal" for "ميتة الحيوان" read as if slaughtered
   meat were included. Now "carrion ... that died without being slaughtered". Ibn Rushd's
   "بأي سبب اتفق أن تذهب حياته" on the pig, which the English had left out, is restored
   ("however it died"). Claim and card.
4. khuff-07/c5: the claim and card said the primers "add" taking the socks off and the end
   of the time to what ends a wudu made with wiping. Al-Quduri and Abu Shuja' say these end
   the wiping, and al-Quduri then has him wash his feet without repeating the rest; only
   'Umdat al-Fiqh says the purification is void. Ibn Rushd records this as a difference in
   the same mas'ala ("واختلفوا هل نزع الخف ناقض لهذه الطهارة أم لا"). Claim and card now say
   the wiping ends and that the effect on the wudu is differed on; the Hanafi position
   carries al-Quduri's next sentence (quote extended; the online copy has the typos "عيه"
   and "بقيه" in the words after the stored quote); the cross-location is recorded.
5. ghusl-05/c4 Hanbali: the position paraphrased the primer's Maymunah narration without
   saying whose washing she described, and skipped "ثم ضرب بيده على الحائط والأرض". Now
   names the Prophet ﷺ and keeps that step.
Read and left as they are:
- wudu-04/c4 Shafi'i: "a woman who is not close kin" for "المرأة الأجنبية". Literal enough;
  the primer does not define the term further.
- tayammum-06/c5: "with water" is not in Ibn al-Mundhir's sentence but follows from "ثم وجد
  الماء"; the card has it without.
- wudu-03/c4, c5 and khuff-07/c4: Ibn Rushd gives Malik's view on each, but al-Risala's
  chapters read here do not state it in a quotable phrase, so no Maliki position is shown
  (positions come from the primer only). The claim text names the difference either way.
- 'Umdat al-Fiqh, ghusl: the online copy reads "وتسن التسمية ويدلك بدنه بيديه" and drops
  "تعميم" before "بدنه بالغسل". Our reading (rubbing recommended; washing the whole body
  obligatory) is what the sentence says with or without the slips.
Limits unchanged: one online copy per work, and a located phrase is not a scholar's reading.
Nothing removed; no item could not be verified.

